: > family will be commercially dead, with quite a high degree of confidence.  If 
: > you can still sell the cheaper chips in mind-boggling quantities, why bother 
: > trying to sell the more expensive ones in competition with them?

: Profit margin. And if the delay of Merced is a clever ploy on Intel's
: part, they've managed to fool the financial analysts who follow
: the industry.

:   -doug

Up until recently, Intel could always rely on fat margins throughout
its processor line, including desktop, mobile, and server chips.  But
now due to competition from AMD and Cyrix, margins in all but the extreme
high end of the desktop segment are being reduced.  To compensate Intel
is keeping prices high where it has little competition (mobile), and
extremely high where there is no competion (SMP servers).  However,
if AMD is successful with the K7 and gets multiprocessor design wins
next year, that market will undergo a dramatic drop in margins.  If
Intel came out with Merced next year, they would have had another
tool to keep the cloners at bay.  

Intel needs to get systems out as soon as possible for
developers to get cranking on real machines.  Otherwise they
give Sun and IBM and whoever else who wishes to compete using 
other high end architectures time to further entrench themselves in
customer accounts and gain large market shares.  

Anyway, if Intel were to really wait until the IA32 family were
dead commercially, they would have to wait another five or six
years minimum.





