In article <357082D0.407D13FD@videotron.ca> JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> writes:
>
>Alpha has been around for YEARS. Intel has be stuck with its 8086 for over a
>decade (perhaps closer to 2).
>
>Alpha has been far superior to the 8086. Yet, the 8086 (now called a Pentium
>MMX PRO II or whatever the marketing-do-jour decides) has supremacy over all
>but a small niche market.
>
>Why would anyone think that a further delay of a non-existent chip cause Alpha
>to suddently take off ? 

  There are a few new things under the sun:
  * Alpha now runs a popular operating system (WNT),
  * Alpha can run many popular applications,
  * Alpha has come down in price to the point that home users can afford it,
  * Alpha has multiple independent motherboard manufacturers, driving down
    motherboard costs.

  What remains to be seen is whether or not Compaq or Samsung will
advertise Alpha.  In the past, Alpha didn't have a chance of grabbing 
significant market share because Digital refused to advertise it.  

  Regardless of the *real* meaning of further delay of a non-existent
chip, it has created in many people's minds the notion of there being
a power vaccuum at the high end of the scale for both workstations and
personal computers, so it's conceivable that they will latch onto the
Alpha as a means to fill that vaccuum.  

  I wouldn't give odds on that happening, though.

  -- Bill Moyer




