Daniel & Laura Burk wrote:
> 
> This is what I have found so far:
> 
> 1) The system will not communicate with the DB-9 console when a color
> console module is present. The system freezes during startup at status
> character "A".  I have no keyboard or color monitor.  I need one! Anyone
> have a spare keyboard lying around? There's also a port with an icon of
> a circle surrounding an arrow on the color cable (by the RGB ports).
> What is it, and what does it connect to?

I'm assuming that you have a BC19 cable.  The mini-DIN (round) connector
is for a mouse.  You need a DEC specific mouse, a PC one won't do. 
However, it is academic until you get the system up and a windowing
system running.  The modular connector is for a VT keyboard.  Normally,
an LK201 was used with these systems, but an LK401 may also work (I
haven't tried this).

If a graphics board is present, the console will try to use the keyboard
on the graphics cable and will not use the console port.  It sounds like
the system is failing the selftest of the graphics system, perhaps
because there is no keyboard?  (Is a mouse necessary to pass this self
test?)

> 2) When the graphics boards are removed, the system communicates over
> the DB9 RS232 line. You can use a PC RS232 port provided you use a
> straight-through DB9S - DB9S cable. Use 8 data bits, 1 stop bit, and
> even parity.

I suspect that a straight through cable will give you trouble if the
system tries to use the handshake lines.  I would wire a correct cable. 
The pinouts are in the VMS FAQ.


> 3) I got the countdown to go to 3 at which time I get the chevron. I
> disabled the autoboot sequence by turning the toggle upwards, to the
> dot-in-circle icon. When the autoboot sequence is enabled, the system
> hangs up at 2..
> 
> 4) When at the chevron, I type " B DUA0", at which time the system hangs
> at 2..
> 
> 5) "B DUA1" yields an error that says something to the effect of file
> not found.

(3) and (4) are the same thing.  It could mean that the disk controller
is not positioned correctly in the backplane and the interrupt grant
chain is broken.  However, (5) indicates that the disk controller can
talk to the disk.  DUA0 may be toast.

 
> Is the hard drive crashed? Can I verify it's status from the chevron?
> Where do I go from here?  The "SHOW" command does not exist. It takes
> the first character which is "S". The second operand seems to be a
> number. Is this a way to set the stack pointer to a certain place?

The MVII has a very primitive console command set.  No HELP, SHOW, or T
50.  The way to go from here is usually to boot the MVII diagnostics
tape.  This can be tough to find and it requires that you TK50 be
working.  Alternately, with Ethernet and a cluster boot host on the
network, you can boot the MVII as a satellite and then look at it.


> The cable routing seems to be fine. Thanks to Megan Gentry for the
> explaination of the Q-Bus.
> 
> Is it time to reformat the drive?  If so, how is this accomplished?
> I've joined DECUS, and am planning on getting the OpenVMS Hobbyist kit.
> I don't have a CD-ROM, however. The TK50 tape drive is untested. I may
> want to  replace the TK50 and RD53's with a SCSI controller and a CD-ROM
> & 1 Gig hard drive. What will it take?  Anyone have a spare?  Same thing
> with memory. There's one M7608 module in this machine: 4 MB RAM?  I'd
> like to expand it out.

Whether you replace the RD53s or not, you may want to keep the TK50 (if
it works) so that you can create a bootable copy of stand alone backup. 
You may not be able to do so with a SCSI controller.

The MVII has 1MB memory on the CPU card.  When testing a system, I
normally take all other memory cards out because the console's memory
test is extremely slow.  Once you get the rest of the machine working,
then put the memory back in.

MVII memory cards usually have 4MB or 8MB.  You can have up to 2 memory
cards.  With 2 8MB cards, you get 16MB (not 17MB) as that is the most
the MVII can address.  So common memory configurations are 5MB, 9MB,
13MB, and 16MB.  (NOTE: To use 16MB, you should have the shielded
version of the PMI (CPU/memory interconnect) cable.  The unshielded
cables tended to produce parity errors, which will crash the system.)

Once you get the system working and know your memory situation, let me
know.  I may be able to get you more memory.

Good luck!

