But isn't it illegal to run unix on a 21164-p8 cpu?  

This is what dec stated in a recent email to me:

The LX motherboard with a full-funcion 21164 (not the NT part), an SRM
console firmware load, and a Digital UNIX license and CD will certainly
run
Digital UNIX.  However this is not a complete answer to your question.

The bundle of the LX motherboard with the P8 processor is intended to be
a
cost effective Windows NT solution kit.  The P8 processor is technically
different from the full-function UNIX 21164s in that it does not support
a
specific memory management mode that UNIX (and VMS) requires and Windows
NT
does not.  From time to time, since the full function part is a full
superset of the NT part, we will mark full function parts as P8s to
fulfill
demand.  As a result sometimes if these are tested they will be found to
be
capable of running Digital UNIX.  However this can not be relied on and
is a
risky proposition if you expect to save money this way.

In addition the Digital UNIX license explicitly spells out what
processors
it may legally run on and the NT parts, including the P8 are not
included in
this list.  As a result anyone intentionally running Digital UNIX on
non-full function marked parts is in violation of their Digital UNIX
software license and at risk of losing the rights to use that product.
Since the number of UNIX licenses is quite traceable to the hardware
that it
is running on, this is probably not a good idea for anyone running an
ethical business.

The bottom line is that it is not a good idea to cheat on processors for
running Digital UNIX.  You will continue to see discussions on the net
about
how this should work but it is simply not a good business practice to
follow.



