Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> http://www.intel.com/pressroom/archive/releases/sp052998.HTM
> 
> Now sampling in 1999 with volume delivery as late as mid-2000.
> 
> -Z-

   So the earth shattering Intel uber-chip slips schedule once again.

   In the words of the taunting school yard bully on the Simspons: ah-ha!

   This certainly puts an interesting new spin on the prospects of Alpha.
   With the imminent release of systems based on the 0.35 um 21264 with
   hopes for 800+ MHz 0.25 um '264s this fall it looks like the Alpha
   might have a cakewalk as performance king to at least 2000 (PA-8500?,
   US-III? give me a break!).  And given the way Mr Pollack back pedalled
   performance expectations on Merced-I at the last uP Forum, I doubt it
   would match up well against a shrunk 21264 even without the delay.

   Now what might the *real* reason for the Merced delay be?

   1) The compiler technology for IA-64 is proving tougher than originally
      thought and results to date are embarassingly pedestrian.

   2) Merced I as originally planned has a serious competitiveness problem
      vis-a-vis the 21264 and maybe even other RISCs (see above).

   3) Merced I die is so bloated with compatibility crap that Intel makes
      tons more bucks per wafer by stretching out x86 lifespan with xeons
      and tanners so the MBA bean counters vetoed the IA-64 rollout schedule.

   4) HP's architecture smarts have rubbed off on Intel and the IA-64 compiler
      output is peforming so well on Merced-I sims that Intel wants to delay
      marketing the Merced-I and imploding the market for all other 64 bit CPUs
      while under the proctoscopic scrutiny of the FTC ("delayed doomsday"
      scenario)

   5) Maybe Intel really is having a bear bringing up its 0.18 um process
      and Merced-I is too big in 0.25 um to fit on a reticle.

   6) Andy can't think of a cute name to follow up celery and zee-on.

   7) HAL won't open the pod bay doors (we are talking almost 2001  ;-)

   8) Maybe there is no delay after all and Intel is preparing for a pearl
      harbour suprise product launch like with the PPro/200.


   What are the possible scenarios that might be played out?

   1) Maybe the Merced-I will never be productized and simply becomes a
      captive software development platform until McKinley (Merced-II)
      can be trotted out to challenge Alpha.

   2) Microsponge is left holding the bag on 64 bit NT.  I get the strong
      impression that M$ had zero interest in delivering NT64 on Alpha.
      Feeling the pressure to play with the Unix big boys, maybe Gates
      will now do right by DEC and deliver first on Alpha (but not holding
      my breath)

   3) Compaq is now left in the driver's seat for delivering NT servers
      with processor performance well above and beyond Intel's Xena
      processor warrior and its tinkertoy chipsets and standard high
      volume boards.

   4) SGI: maybe discarding the MIPS "beast" in a headless chicken panic over
      the bunny suiter's scary 64 bit vapour chip wasn't the best move.  Of
      course, SGI hasn't had the best luck in making strategic decisions
      since passing up deep sixing starfire a while back. Better follow
      up on those Alpha T3E supercomputers SGI!

   5) Maybe Merced-I does ship in 2000 as planned but comes up against
      el cheapo 21264PCs and the first generation of 21364 Alpha devices.

   6) Market is wide open for Alpha but Samsung proves too sick with the
      Asian flu to carry through.  Or maybe the FTC wishful thinking comes
      to realization and  Alpha gets early access to copper metalization
      through IBM fabs and gets another leg up for the race to 1 GHz and
      beyond.
      

Here's to DEC: may they for once miss an opportunity to miss an opportunity
for succeeding with Alpha!

Paul

All opinions strictly my own (obviously).
   
--
Paul W. DeMone                 The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race
Kanata, Ontario                to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com              architectures with MIPSed results but
PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net    ALPHA's well that ends well.




