Anil T Maliyekke (amaliy1@uic.edu) wrote:

: How would one make a 21264PC?  It doesn't seem like there is a 
: straight forward way to pare down the 21264.  And considering
: that the 21264 still hasn't shipped, why should one expect
: the Alpha design team to get another more complex processor
: design out in that time frame?

Cut it down to three way superscalar (2 x integer + 1 x floating point).
That would greatly simplify the instruction decode, simplify the floating
point register file (less ports), remove one of the two integer register
files and the complex logic to keep them synchonised, and generally
simplify the monster and cut down the die size so that someone might
actually be able to build it in commercial quantities.

Keep the internal cache as it is or perhaps reduce it slightly to
64KI+32KD. Keep almost all the functional units. Lose two of the integer
ALUs, but probably keep everything else. Functional units are a small part
of the die in a monster like this anyway. Offer it in both 21164PC and
21264 pinouts. And maybe increase the page size to 64K, giving less TLB
misses, and perhaps reducing time to first level cache by one clock.

Performance should be down a bit but perhaps SPECINT would not suffer
much. How often does a 21264 actually run at 6 instructions per clock for
real programs anyway? SPECFP is supposed to be closer to memory limited,
so it should go almost as fast in the same package, but slower in the
cheaper 21164PC package.

Of course, if you want to offer a 21264PC with much lower design and
setup costs, you just repackage a 21164PC or 21164 in a 21264 pinout, or
at least with a new label on the chip.



