In article <Pine.SOL.3.96.980523174705.19916A-100000@sci.fi>,
Vesa Kukkonen  <velli@sci.fi> wrote:

>I got a Digital VT1300 X-terminal. When I put it on and say "boot" this
>appears on screen:
>
>-ESA0
>?54 RETRY
>?54 RETRY
>
>etc..
>
>I'm using thin ethernet. I also got a VXT2000, which works perfectly.
>What's wrong with VT1300? I'm using a Linux-box with bootpd as server.
>Btw, will the same image work with VT1300 and VXT2000 ?

The VT1300 is quite a different beast than the VXT2000.  The VT1300 is VAX
based for one, and I think the VXT2000 is RISC based (MIPS perhaps?).  The
VT1300 uses DEC's MOP protocol to load the terminal software it requires. 
Fortunately there is a freely available MOP server for the ranks of Linux
and many of the BSD derived free OSes. So if you can get a copy of the
software image for the VT1300, you can MOP load it from your Linux box
over ethernet.

The VT1300 itself is for all practical purposes a stripped down DEC
VAXstation 3100, retargeted for use as a windowing terminal.  The main
difference between a VAXstation 3100 and a VT1300 is that the VT1300
doesn't come with a disk I/O daughter card.  If you get one, either the
dual SCSI card or the MFM/SCSI card, you can actually add it in and use
the VT1300 with disk drives.  

Or if you're not able to find the VT1300 software, you can always boot
NetBSD/vax on the machine.  The support for the VS3100 isn't extensive
yet, but you can netboot them to NetBSD/vax and have a nice little
diskless Unix workstation.  Besides, you'll have bragging rights to say
you've got your own VAX.

For details see,  http://world.std.com/~bdc/projects/vaxen/ 
and the NetBSD homepage,  http://www.netbsd.org/

>And another question, can I use my VRC16 monitor with PC somehow?

Probably.  I've seen some places that carry the necessary converters to
adapt VGA out to the various DEC monitor formats, but the converters
usually cost more than an equivalent or superior new monitor. 

-brian.
