Brian JARAI Chase wrote:

> The VT1300 is quite a different beast than the VXT2000.  The VT1300 is VAX
> based for one, and I think the VXT2000 is RISC based (MIPS perhaps?).  The

VXT2000 has a VAX inside it. Sure it is different as it can do MOP V3, MOP V4,
BOOTBP/TFTP boots, it has more NVRAM. The CPU has more horsepower etc.
But still it is good old VAX.

And VT1300 can run same image that VXT2000 runs. VXT software comes in two
major
flavors

* "host based", booted from VMS, UNIX (HP/SUN/AIX just name it) host via
MOP or BOOTP. This VT1300 cannot really run, or to be precise can run but will

crash as soon as you modify just about ANY SETTING

* Infoserver based, a small VXTLDR.SYS is MOP or BOOTBP loaded and
then VXT connects to Infoserver using Lastport/Disk protocol. Infoserver and
VXT sort
of "cluster" and Infoserver acts as paging storage for VXT. VT1300 can use
this flavor.

There is also third flavor, VXT-EX but I never played with that one.

_veli




