In article <6jnju5$k99$1@news.ro.com>,
	gheston@ro.com (Gary Heston) writes:
> There's a lot of empty space in there, making it easier to work on but
> consequently much bigger outside. About 1/4 of the rack height is used
> for cooling duct and fan--the card cage itself is no more than 12"
> high. The power supplies are all on top, and there's a slot on the 
> bottom for one H-7440 drive (never have found out what Rnnn type they
> are--supposed to be 660MB, have 14" platters). The card cage has one
> XMI backplane and two BI backplanes.

Interesting design.  Sounds like it's getting on for mainframe status
(okay, before anyone starts, I *know* it's a mini, albeit a large one)
where the current definition seems to be "can you get lost inside it" :)

> Some of the old ('60s and '70s) mainframes did have plumbing running 
> to the cabinets, to get enough cooling in there. 

I think that a lot of them still do; there's the aforementioned ICL
whatever, which needs distilled water to cool it, and I understand that
the current S/370 and S/390 systems are watercooled.  Where I work we
have a leftover from an IBM mainframe or mini (I don't know if it was
an S/3x0 or an old AS/400) in the shape of a large (ie 3x3x5 feet) tape
unit which is water cooled, a fact I discovered after burning myself on
the outlet pipe!

I'm sure I heard somewhere that even the VAX9000 series was intended
to be a water-cooled system (hence the sames Aquarius and AquaVAX IIRC)
but was converted to an air-cooled system somewhere in the design stage.

> If you want large, modern computers, I had a chance to take a tour of
> a secure computer room last Wednesday. They had four Cray systems in
> there, including a 128-node multiprocessor setup that filled nine
> 19" racks. There were a couple of Alpha 7100 systems used to feed them.
> Neat setup,

Sounds like it was pretty interesting.  Do they still do the doughnut
shaped systems with the "seats" around the base?  I think that the
biggest system I worked on was a data matching system which took up
17 full height racks, which was quite impressive, although it was
dwarfed by an(other) ICL mainframe owned by the same organisation
which filled a room that was over half the size of a football pitch.
I wouldn't want to pay the electricity bill!

Just out of interest, who owns the Crays, and what are they used for?
I sometimes get the impression that the various meteorological centres
seem to have something of a monopoly on these systems!

> even if they did tend to talk casually about having recently
> "scrapped a XMP 2". Apparently it got hauled out to a disposal field
> and left out in the rain...

...?!  Sorry, there's not much I can say in reply to that.

> No basement or second story. I do plan to brace the floor where it'll
> be sitting, though.

Probably a good plan!

> I'm not sure about the exact weight, but once I 
> had the cabinets separated, I tried to lift one side of the computer
> cabinet--couldn't even budge it.

I seem to remember that those things' weights are measured in tonnes,
although offhand I have absolutely no idea how many.  On a slightly
unrelated note, is this one of the beasties that needs a 3 phase 415
volt power input?

> I know someone who was running a 11/780 in his garage, he recently 
> upgraded to a MV3200. He told me several years ago that he had a
> 360/44, but hadn't hooked it up.

Hmm, never considered it might be the same person.  :)  I also heard
a rumour of someone who had a PDP10 of some description up and running
in their garage; now that's *really* unusual!

Chris.



