In article <35587FBA.8DDAC3B1@ibm.net>, Mark Reinhart <mrrein@ibm.net> writes:
:    I am interested in getting a VAX. I have several questions that I
:want answered.

  I am assuming hobbyist use here...

:1. I want to run NetBSD and OpenVMS. What is the fastest Vax that will
:do this?

  Most any VAX or MicroVAX will boot OpenVMS.  (There are a few that have
  been discontinued over the years, but you're unlikely to want to use most
  of these discontinued systems for anything other than getting some compute
  cycles out of your electric central heating system. :-)

:2. On a machine without a graphics card, would it be possible to run the
:DecWindows client and use an X-terminal as a display? Could I do this in
:NetBSD with X-windows?

  Under OpenVMS, yes.  However, you will probably end up with a workstation
  anyway -- both for price, for flexibility, for power usage, and for
  considerations of physical size.

  I tend to recommend the VAXstation 4000 series for folks that want a VAX
  -- from the VAXstation 4000 VLC, the VAXstation 4000 model 60, or one of
  the various members of the VAXstation 4000 model 90 series.  The boxes
  other than the VLC can (or do) have a TURBOchannel, and all have SCSI,
  Ethernet, and graphics capabilities standard.

  The VAXstation 3100 series has a 1.073 GB limitation on the maximum system
  disk size, and only the fastest of the VAXstation 3100 series (model 76)
  is faster than the slowest member of the VAXstation 4000 series (the VLC).

  A hobbyist license for OpenVMS VAX V6.1 is available via US DECUS,
  additional information is available via http://www.montagar.com/.
  And V6.1 supports all VAXstation 3100 and VAXstation 4000 systems.

  As for answers your NetBSD questions, you'll want to ask somebody
  more familiar with what systems and configurations NetBSD supports.
  (I *think* I remember references to NetBSD on the VAXstation 4000
  workstations, but you *will* want to confirm this information.)

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
  note to those folks not contributing spam -- there is no ZZ in my address




