:
:This would be KFMSA, board number T2036-AA, correct?
..
:Depends on what we find above.

  In addition to Bob Kaplow's questions and comments....

  Warning: my DSSI and my calypso console is very rusty...

  In addition to Bob Kaplows comments and questions, remember to
  set FORCEUNI to 0 in each disk, and to set a unique non-zero
  UNITNUM value -- it's best to set the UNITNUM value to 8 or
  higher, to prevent any collisions with non-FORCEUNI ISEs.  Then
  -- if there is any chance you will be serving these disks, or if
  you will ever dual-host the DSSI, or if you plan to use volume
  shadowing -- remember to set the ISE parameter ALLCLASS and the
  host ALLOCLASS to the same (non-zero) value.  (You can also set
  cute and unique ISE NODENAME values while you're making adjustments
  in the ISEs; while using the PARAMS utility.)

  Off the top, I don't remember the VAX 6000 console syntax to SET
  HOST into the ISE PARAMS subsystem.  The console command syntax
  tends to look something like one of the following commands:

    >>> SET HOST/DUP/UQSSP/DISK <dssi node id> PARAMS
    P00>>> set host -dup <device_name>

  Please check your system documentation for details and specifics.

  LFU, the loadable firmware utility, can also be involved if you
  need to adjust KFMSA parameters on various VAX processors.

  When OpenVMS is running and FYDRIVER is loaded, the DCL command
  SET HOST/DUP works nicely.  (See the DCL documentation for the
  SET HOST/DUP command for details on connecting FYDRIVER.)

  And if you change any allocation-class or related parameters, you
  may (will) end up rebooting the system (and the VMScluster) to
  pick up the new settings.

