WHITHER DECUS?

Commentary from the May 29 issue of Shannon Knows DEC

(cl) 1998 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC

CopyLefted... redistribute at will


Massive layoffs, the future of Digital�s strategic OSes and RISC
architecture, and the generous glide ratios of the golden parachutes
granted to DEC�s senior managers dominates Usenet discussions and trade
press �news� regarding the coming Compaq-tion. Very little has been said
about the fate of the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society.

As stated previously in this forum, Compaq currently does not sponsor a
user group. Compaq acquisitee Tandem Computers has its International
Tandem Users Group (ITUG), but this fee-based organization differs in
many respects from the DECUS model.

Given the imminent consummation of the merger, Compaq�s plate is pretty
full these days. Nevertheless, the firm is not completely ignorant of
DECUS�Com- paq executive John Rose was active in DECUS during his tenure
as the manager of Digital�s PC business�and the user society has been an
�item of interest� in recent discussions in Houston. Indeed, the extent
of Compaq�s involvement in the upcoming DECUS event in Los Angeles
should become known in the reasonably near future. 

The long-term future of DECUS remains a matter of conjecture. Despite
the fact that Eckhard Pfeiffer runs a lean and mean organization, I
would argue that DECUS represents a tremendous marketing opportunity for
Team Houston. In fact, given that enterprise rivals IBM, HP, Sun
Microsystems, et al, sponsor user groups, logic dictates that Compaq
Computer Corporation will follow suit.

In my role as a purveyor of analysis and information�unsolicited or
otherwise�I would strongly suggest that Compaq embrace DECUS, tweaking
the Society where necessary. Rebranding the Society as the Distributed
Enterprise Computing Users Society might be a good place to start:
there�s nothing like recycling an acronymn that has strong brand equity!

If DECUS is important to you�it certainly is critical to my
business�there are a number of steps you can take. Join a LUG, ante up
the $40 to become a sustaining DECUS member, and monitor comp.org.decus
on Usenet. And you can always show your support by attending the DECUS
�98/Los Angeles event scheduled for October 3-8, 1998. The symposium
features ~30 one- and two-day weekend seminars, as well as 17 roomsful
of concurrent sessions running from Monday morning through Thursday
afternoon. The agenda is not yet finalized, but SKD is confident that
DECUS �98/ Los Angeles will be the first venue during which the tactics,
strategies, and product plans of the Compaq combine will be fully
articulated. This in itself should be ample reason to line up, sign up,
and register today. As with the past 28 consecutive DECUS US Chapter
symposia, SKD will be on hand to share insightful (if irreverent)
commentary on things IT-related). Details can be had by calling
1-800-DECUS55 or by visiting http://www.decus.org/la98/.

Hope to see you in LA this October. As the saying goes, �Be there or be
square.�




