Laurence Blunt wrote:

> There is no such thing as NT, Unix or OpenVMS cpu's, but it depends on
> what firmware is supported on you motherboard.
> 
Actaully, this is not true.  Digital used to charge different prices for
it's CPU's.  For example, for NT or Linux, the price of the CPU's were
"X" and the Unix and O/VMS were "Y".  This recently changed when Samsung
started producing CPU's. . .

> NT uses ARC or AlphaBIOS, and Unix and VMS use SRM. Many systems
> will hold both and permit switching between modes from console commands.
Correct. . .however, the PC164LX motherboards cannot hold both BIO's in
flash proms, so it would require reflashing the BIOs to support Alpha
Bios and SRM console.
> 
> There is one other area you must look at,  most Alpha systems sold for use
> with NT have video and SCSI cards that are not suported by Unix. For example
> most Alpha NT systems have Matrox Millennuim video and Adaptec SCSI,
> neither of which are supported by DEC Unix.
> 
This is correct.  If you want a system that easier switches between
Digital Unix and Windows NT or Linux, use - for video S3 Trio, ATI Mach
32, ATI Mach 64 For SCSI, use a Symbios 53C810 or 53C875 based card,
for  Ethernet use a Digital DE500.  Check also our card for SCSI and
Ethernet.  (Http://www.digitalscape.com).  Our card runs WinNT and Linux
and sells for $200 or so depending on were you get it.  There's another
card from Intraserver that runs all the O/S's for SCSI and Ethernet.  I
believe their card is a bit more though.

Bruce Faust
