In article <w4ozpgm7wxj.fsf@irtnog.org>, "His Holiness the Reverend Doktor Xenophon Fenderson, the writes:
> 	- Ehud Gavron, Chad Maloney, and Roger Ivie expand this
> 	  explanation, stating that not only is the 9-pin printer
> 	  port a serial port, but that there are also two more serial
> 	  ports embedded in the video console connector, for a total
> 	  of three serial ports: 

There are _four_ serial ports. The 25-pin port is also a serial port.

> 	- Mr. Maloney and Mr. Gavron go on to say that the 25-pin
> 	  connector is used to connect a BA40 expansion box.  The BA40
> 	  has connectors for a TK50 drive and for the old-style "3
> 	  rows of pins" SCSI disk connectors for RD54/RD53/RD52 hard
> 	  disk drives (this style of connector is also common on old
> 	  Sun workstations, in my experience).  Without the expansion
> 	  unit, I will be unable to connect other SCSI devices to this
> 	  machine.

This is incorrect. The 25-pin D connector to which you have access while
the machine is in the box is a serial port. There are several internal
connectors which get cabled to the expansion box (among these is a 24? pin
connector for the second MFM drive; perhaps this is what has given rise to
the confusion). The expansion box gives you a large D connector (50-pin?) 
for a second MFM drive as well as a 50-pin Centronix-style connector for SCSI. 

> 	- What's a BCC08 cable?

It's just like a BCC05, but it shorts pins 8 and 9. :-)

It's a 9-pin to 25-pin cable that shorts pins 8 and 9 of the 9-pin connector.
You can't use a PC 9- to 25- cable because DEC used a different pinout than
IBM did. I don't have anything with DEC's pinout handy, so I can't tell you
which pins you need.
> 
> 	- I have a 25-pin-to-Centronics cable, a SCSI CD-ROM drive, an
> 	  external SCSI enclosure (which is the reason for the
> 	  Centronics adapter), and the OpenVMS Hobbyist CD-ROM from
> 	  Montagar Software Concepts.  I'm getting the feeling that my
> 	  idea of plugging the CD-ROM drive into the VS2000 and
> 	  booting the OpenVMS installer that way isn't going to work.
> 	  Is this the case?

Yes, for a number of reasons.

The 25-pin connector is a serial port, not a SCSI port. You would have to
cable from the internal 50-pin SCSI connector.

The VS2000 doesn't know how to boot a SCSI CD-ROM.
