> Eric Osborne <Eric.D.Osborne@digital.com> wrote

> Let me explain. Consider, for instance, the
> typical ISV. They will more often than not
> (at least if they're sane) produce their product
> from a single code base. Since they want the code
> base to be as consistent as possible across platforms,
> that generally implies that the "64-bit" versions
> on DU are simple re-compiles, which pick up the
> ability to use larger amounts of memory in the
> form of big linearly addressed buffers.
>
> Now, this is a useful thing, but is by no
> means the only - or perhaps not even the most
> important - place where performance gains are
> realized.
>
> The bigger hits come in the form of new
> algorithms that exploit the larger address
> space. That's where straight recompiles of
> 32 bit code fail to take advantage of the
> architecture, and is where many benchmarks
> fail - because THE CODE IS SPECIFIED AS A
> PART OF THE TEST, AND THE CODE IS OPTIMIZED
> FOR 32 BIT SYSTEMS. It's rather like racing
> an airplane against a car, but forcing the
> plane to stay on the ground. Hardly a true test
> of the real performance.
>

This is as close to the dog ate my homeworktype of excuse that I have seen yet.

TPC-C and TPC-D make NO SUCH 32 bit
assumption, you can implement the queries
in any way you like using what ever database
architecture you like. Your assertion that
they are biased towards 32 bit systems is
SIMPLY UNTRUE, they are a just set of standard
queries.

Even with SAP and Baan which may be 32 bit
applications you can choose to locate tables
in memory if you feal that it will boost your
performance. In particular Baan BRU has a
relatively small Database which would mostly
fit in memory.

You really seem to be suggesting that despite
all the hype surrounding VLM 64 the reallity
is that Oracle etc don't make use of the 64 bit
features of DU that would really make a difference.

This seems to be almost the same has saying
that 64 bit DU has no value for 99% of all database
applications I am sure this is not what you ment.

> Real customers aren't constrained by a set of
> artificial rules from modifying their applications.
> So they can, and do, make changes that DO use
> 64 bit abilities.
>

But not if they are using 32 bit applicationswhich according to you most are. Doesn't
this rather marginalise any benefits that
VLM 64/64bit DU  may infer.

> On a different front, many customers have relatively small
> databases (say 6-10GB), but VERY high transaction volumes.
> I've seen a customr get more than 16,000 queries per second
> out of a single 4-processor node when using a fully-cached
> database. You just can't do that when you have to go to disk.
>

Very interesting but its a shame that Digital don't seemto be able to follow their own advice. Baan uses a small
database <10 GB in total, its an OLTP application but
there is no evidence of Digital benefiting from this
in the light of their current Baan BRU numbers.

The probable reason is that Baan needs other memory
resources for connection management etc. This means
that the 8400 requirment to trade memory slots with
I/O and CPUs means that actually putting the Baan
database in memory is impractical.

> When they do, they get performance that just can't be touched
> by 32 bit systems. As mainstream database vendors take the leap,
> with the implied deviation between 32 and 64 bit flavors of their
> products, the 32 bit systems will be inevitably left behind.
>

But you are unwilling to share actual examples. Thereally interesting thing is that the UltraSPARC VIS
instruction set delivers much more real performance
benefit to customers that 64 bit does to DU.

I can actually produce real examples where VIS
speeds up applications to a level that no Alpha
can touch. Practical examples are Gene sequencing
Some FFT's, Linear arithmetic, sorts, Photoshop,
Solaris IP stack processing etc etc.

> And I'm sure, Andrew, that is why Sun - and HP, and IBM -
> are spending vast sums of R&D money trying to implement
> technology that you keep saying is unimportant.
>

Oh no I think you missed the point. 64 bit is importantyes it will deliver performance benefits. No one
doubts that, but what is really evident is that
due to a number of very practical reasons like
the fact that the 8400 can have loads of CPU and
memory and I/O but not all at the same time these
benefits have been hyped by Digital but not realised
by Digitals customers.

Yes when Sun has a fully 64 bit Solaris we will
market it like we marketed VIS. But like VIS
64 bit Solaris will actually have a practical
value to customers because we have platforms
that can realise its benefits which currently
Digital don't. E.g We can put 28 CPUs 28
GB of RAM and more I/O than the whole
8400 backplane into an E6000 all at the
same time or 64 CPUs 64 GB and the I/O
bandwidth of 6 8400 backplanes into a E10000.

