I'm setting up a number of DECservers for TCP/IP.  We have a mix of
DECserver 700's and DECserver 90TL's.  The DS700's I'm very familiar
with and they are working just fine.  We have an NT 4.0 server w/SP3
configured as our DHCP server and we are using a shareware TFTP service
on the same NT server.  The DS700's are booting v2.2 of WWENG2.SYS and
are obtaining their IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, domain
name and DNS server addresses from the DHCP server.  Each terminal
server has a reservation made for its own ethernet address.  The
DS90TL's are obtaining an IP address, but they are failing to get a
subnet mask, default gateway, domain name and DNS server address.  The
DS90TL's are running v1.1c of MNENG1.SYS.  Network sniffer analysis
indicates that all of this information is in the DCHP reply that is sent
to the terminal server.  In fact, the only differences between the
replies sent to the DS700's and the DS90TL's are the IP address, client
name and client hardware address; these differences are to be expected.

Does anybody out there have any experience with the 90TL's and booting
them via BOOTP/DHCP and  TFTP server?  Is there any way to get these
units obtain ALL of their IP configuration data from the DHCP server?
It is important as we will be putting these devices into remote
locations where it will not be possible to connect via NCP and they will
have to be able to get all of their IP configuration dynamically w/o any
user intervention.

Please respond via email as well as usenet post.

TIA,

Chuck
