 hello,


>        On the back of the VAXstation 2000, there are two ports, a
>        9-pin male DIN with a picture of a printer above it, and a
>        25-pin male DIN with two arrows above it.  Which one might be
>        the serial port?

 they both are. The one with the arrows is the 'communications' port, 
and the other is as youd educed the 'printer' port. With the serial console
(theres a switch for it) enabled, the console will be found on the printer
port. 

>I suspect the serial port is the 9-pin connector and the 25-pin
>connector is for external disk drives (SCSI? MASSBUS?).

nope. no SCSI for this poor beast. The connector that _looks_ like a SCSI
connector but isnt is only really useful to connect a TKZ50. It isnt 100%
SCSI. There was once a lot of folklore about people managing to get SCSI
devices working on it but ive never seen it done.
 
>        I'm assuming that 70MBytes is enough room for some kind of
>        minimal VMS installation, maybe even DECwindows.  Assuming
>        that this is enough space, and assuming that these boxes have
>        enough RAM to run VMS, how could I get VMS onto this machine?

 Either TK50's or floppies, assuming you had an RX50. 
If you have another VMS machine on your network you could bring it up
as a diskless node in a cluster. 

happy hacking,
gregg





