In article <35589F27.9528B4A7@Eng.Sun.Com>,
ASHOK SINGHAL  <ashok.singhal@Eng.Sun.Com> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>> 
>> However, it seems to me that "most" individual apps will fit in
>> local memory, especially if the machine is running on the order
>
>Depends on how you count "most".  

Maybe I am "estimating" instead of counting?  My 6-year-old
tells me that "estimating" is the same as "guessing", which
is closely related to "arguing by anecdotal evidence", which
is what both Ashok and I are doing here.

It is important to remember a couple of things when thinking
about the "performance penalties" of off-node memory references
in a ccNUMA machine like the Origin:

(1) Many apps are not significantly memory-access-limited, so an
    increase in latency from ~300ns to ~600-700ns for a fraction
    of the cache misses will have a performance impact that is 
    significantly smaller than the increase in average latency.

    Given that we are comparing machines with different cpus, 
    different O/S's, different compilers, different prices, and
    (often) different platform-optimized source code, the effect
    of a small latency increase will be way down in the noise
    for these apps.  

    Of course, this includes both the latency increase for off-node
    accesses in a ccNUMA machine (like the SGI Origin2000) as well
    as the increase in latency for building a large, monolithic
    shared-memory machine like the Ultra10000 or the DEC 8400.

(2) Many of the apps that are significantly memory-access-limited
    are bandwidth-limited, rather than latency-limited.  In this
    case, you need to look at the sustainable bandwidth numbers,
    not the latency numbers.  They are related, but not in an 
    easy way.  For example, the Sun Ultra6000 runs STREAM Copy a
    bit slower than the Origin2000 for local accesses, but faster
    than the Origin if one or both of the arguments to STREAM Copy
    are non-local.  On the other hand, the extra latency of the 
    Sun Ultra10000 decreases the sustainable bandwidth for STREAM
    Copy so that the Origin2000 is faster even if both the source
    and destination memory blocks are on the most distant node of
    the machine.  This is the contention-free result.   

    Results under heavy contention will certainly vary, but it is
    not possible to make blanket statements, except that the Sun
    Ultra 10000 will generally scale very well under heavy
    contention, while the Origin2000 scaling will be somewhat more
    dependent on the details of the access patterns.   This is
    "simple physics" associated with the slightly lower-bandwidth
    links on the Origin2000 relative to the Sun Ultra10000 nodes,
    and the fact that the Origin2000 interconnect fabric is a
    bristled hypercube instead of a fully connected multi-stage
    switch.

(3) The remaining apps that are memory-access-limited and latency-
    limited fall into several more sub-categories, depending on the
    size of the working set and the distribution of accesses across
    that working set. Those with either small working sets or with
    concentration of accesses in a relatively small region can work
    fine on a ccNUMA machine (especially with the node memories
    configured to some large value, like 4 GB).  On the other hand,
    those with very large working sets and uniformly distributed
    references will show performance characteristics more closely
    approximating the hypothetical average latency function.

    I am not arguing that this last set is unimportant, since it
    does include things like TPC-C and some important codes run
    by the intelligence community, but I am saying that it is 
    necessary to understand the memory access characteristics 
    of your own applications in order to understand how well it
    is likely to work on a given memory architecture.  

    No vendor has produced a machine that is uniformly superior to
    all other vendors' machines for all application areas -- the
    economics of the technologies and the requirement for engineering
    trade-offs do not allow that to happen.


> 1. You can have situations where the sum of all the application
> memory is greater than the total memory available, but memory is
> still not oversubscribed.  That's because even applications
> implicitly share memory even if they are completely
> single-threaded.  An example of this is shared libraries, and its
> very likely that libraries such as libc, libX11 etc are shared
> extensively.  

Text accesses to shared libraries are almost never in the critical
path for performance.  Access times are much more strongly determined
by how clever the loader is at getting shared-library accesses 
started early and by the efficiency of the file system at loading
the data than by the latency of getting the individual cache
lines from the buffer to which the file system loads the library.


> 3. Non-local memory may result, even if there is plenty of
> memory, as a result of load balancing.  

Yes, this is a "feature", not a "bug".  The scheduler will keep
track of the "home" node for a job, and run it there as often as
possible.  When that node becomes congested, it will run the
process on a nearby node, rather than allowing the congestion to
build up (which is what happens on a cluster, since process
migration is very expensive, and generally not available there).

It is correct that this is more complex than on a flat SMP
machine, and can result in some performance degradation relative
to a hypothetical machine with a flat, low latency.  However, 
that hypothetical machine with flat, low latency does not actually
exist, so the right thing to do is workload evaluation of the real
machines to see how well the balances of cpu performance and 
the hierarchies of memory latency and bandwidth work at each
price point for the workload that the customer needs to run.




>Specrate runs N copies of a benchmark, waits for all copies to complete,
>runs N copies of another benchmark, etc.  Typically N is the same as
>the number of CPUs, there are no other significant runnable jobs 
>and there is consequently no load-balancing at all.  There is also
>no memory pressure and the applications block very little.

SPECrate certainly has limitations as a "workload" benchmark.
It does have one redeeming property, and that is that it shows
clearly the reduction in performance due to contention for a 
shared memory bandwidth resource by multiple, independent jobs.
This is a real effect, and one that users ought to be aware of.
Given the fact that the SPECrate infrastructure already exists,
it is probably a good idea to keep it for SPEC98.  

On the other hand, there is no reason to limit our thinking about
"throughput" or "workload" benchmarks to the form taken by SPECrate.
I believe that both computer vendors and customers would be well-
served by the development of a more realistic throughput benchmark
based on the individual SPEC codes.  This would likely include both 
compiling and running the codes, but would have ordering and
synchronization properties that would be mostly chaotic (like real
systems), rather than lock-step like the current SPECrate benchmark.
