:)  The 25 pin is a "comm" or terminal port.  The 9pin is the console port.
A BCC08 cable connected to the 9pin and to a terminal will gain you access 
to the console.

> Question Zwei:
> 
>         I'm assuming that 70MBytes is enough room for some kind of
>         minimal VMS installation, maybe even DECwindows.  Assuming
>         that this is enough space, and assuming that these boxes have
>         enough RAM to run VMS, how could I get VMS onto this machine?
> 
> I don't have a tape drive, and I'm assuming that the VAXstation 2000
> doesn't have SCSI (at least, that's what the relevant FAQs would lead
> me to believe).  Could I load the files over via MOP or NFS?  Would I
> have to boot the VAXstation from another VMS node and install that
> way?

70 megs is small.  Especially for VMS and DECwindow.  I'd look about and
see if you can find a RD54 (Maxtor XT2190).  Shouldn't cost you too much
though, being they are old and MFM, they still might cost about $200(US).


The microVAX 2000 had an expansion option which was a plate on the bottom
of the machine about 1.25 inches in height.  It allowed for the back plate
to have a 50 pin telco/SCSI  style connector for a tape drive and a 50(?)
pin three tier D connector for an external drive.  Internally, there is a
small PCB with a 74xxx series line transceiver chip (74240??).  If you've
got such a beast, you can connect a tape drive but it sounds as if you will
have to go the "cluster satellite" route to get anything onto the drive.

> I'd really like to run OpenVMS on this machine, as I already have a
> plethora of UNIX boxes at home.  :)

I'd gladly put VMS 6.1 on the drive for you from the Hobbyist CD if you
get yourself a Hobbyist license.  You'd have to get ahold of an RD54 or
XT2190 though.  I've done this for a few folks already.  Contact me at 
the address in the SIG if you'd like to approach it this way.

