In article <w4olnsa3qhx.fsf@irtnog.org>, econommx@rose-hulman.edu writes...
#I'm having trouble figuring out several things related to the
#VAXstation 2000.  I currently have two working VAXstation 2000s, one
#of which has a 70MByte hard disk in it.  (The diskless machine runs
#NetBSD/vax 1.3 and boots across the network from my Linux file
#server.)
# 
#Question Numero Uno:
# 
#        On the back of the VAXstation 2000, there are two ports, a
#        9-pin male DIN with a picture of a printer above it, and a
#        25-pin male DIN with two arrows above it.  Which one might be
#        the serial port?

The printer port is _a_ serial port.
The video port _can_have_a_ serial port.
You can get the magic little box from dec that plugs into both and
gives you 3 mmj outs (TTA0,1,2).

# 
#I suspect the serial port is the 9-pin connector and the 25-pin
#connector is for external disk drives (SCSI? MASSBUS?).

No, there is a BA40 expansion box you COULD have on the bottom,
which would give you SCSI (AMP) for TK50s, or the 3-rows-of-pins
D-connector for RD54s/53s/52s.

# 
#Question Zwei:
# 
#        I'm assuming that 70MBytes is enough room for some kind of
#        minimal VMS installation, maybe even DECwindows.  Assuming
#        that this is enough space, and assuming that these boxes have
#        enough RAM to run VMS, how could I get VMS onto this machine?

You can fit a good working VMS system on 35Mb.  You can't fit DECwindows
on there and expect to run.  (e.g. Logs, Pagefiles, output files, etc.)

# 
#I don't have a tape drive, and I'm assuming that the VAXstation 2000
#doesn't have SCSI (at least, that's what the relevant FAQs would lead

Then you don't have a BA40 below it, which means you're limited to the
one drive inside it.

#me to believe).  Could I load the files over via MOP or NFS?  Would I
#have to boot the VAXstation from another VMS node and install that
#way?

You can't build VMS via either NFS or MOP.  YOur best bet
is to boot it into a cluster, or plug its disk into something
that can also talk to it.

