No doubt this is true but this is not what I mean.
UNIX clusters are also common and they are mainly
used to provide HA access to resources.
Clusters to provide scalability which is what Digital
are suggesting is possible with their TPC-C press
releases are much more rare. This is also the
case with OpenVMS database clusters. You
know the scenario where 2 nodes accessing
the database are 2 x faster than 1.

HA clustering is much easier to implement. Since
it does not inflict any DLM overhead on the system
only recovery traffic, it also does not require any data
partitioning.

> I would certainly believe that Unix clusters would have
> less of a following, because they are less feature-complete.
>

This is true in the sense that UNIX clusters don't
havethings like a cluster filesystem. It is not true if you
are operating at the Oracle/Sybase/Informix level
since they all implement their own shared resources
mechanism.

The UNIX platform vendors are left to provide cluster
managment. cluster wide volume management and DLM's.

> Even if DEC Unix reaches the point of cluster parity with
> VMS, I think their clustering percentage will be less than
> for VMS, since Unix afficiandos are accustomed to products
> named "clustering" which provide just a shadow of the full
> capabilities of VMS clusters.
>

This may or may not be true, however it isn't the point.
The issues of getting Oracle/Informix or Sybase to scale
is the same on DU or OpenVMS.

OpenVMS may give you facilites like Cluster Filesystems
which DU does not but Oracle etc don't use the filesystem.


