John Laird wrote:
> 
> In article <BACB7CC43C716662.E6399CDC4AD2960A.DF8567CDF8D330E0@library-proxy.airnews.net>, Chris Scheers <asi@airmail.net> writes:
> > Roy Omond wrote:
> >>
> >> I'm sure this has come up in the past...
> >>
> >> I have inherited a couple of VAXstation 4000 VLC's with no memory.
> >> Looking at the last Systems and Options Catalogue where the VLC was
> >> featured, it looks like the memory type (MS40-AB) is fairly vanilla 80ns
> >>
> >> 72-pin SIMMs.
> >>
> >> The price asked for real second-hand MS40-AB's from some of the usual
> >> DEC used equipment resellers in the UK is ridiculous.  So ...
> >>
> >> what kind of "PC" (yeah, I know ...) memory could I use in place of the
> >> real thing ?  I seem to recall there being an issue with e.g. 70 ns
> >> stuff.
> >
> >
> > The VS4000-VLC is generic, 72 pin parity memory.  Unfortunately, only
> > 4MB SIMMs may be used, so 24MB is the maximum memory.  We have 70ns
> > SIMMs in our machine.
> >
> 
> I understood that generic 4MB SIMMs were typically single-sided.  The
> (non-Digital, but supplied as compatible) Siemens and Samsung boards in
> my VLC are both double-sided, as are genuine MS40-AB parts.  Those in the
> know reckon that this sort of difference gives a typical 486 no end of
> headaches.  Am I right in thinking that you believe both styles of board
> will work in a VLC ?

Single/Double sided isn't really an issue.  Single/Double bank is an
issue for 72 pin SIMMs.

Generally, 1/4/16/64MB SIMMs are single bank and 2/8/32/128MB SIMMs are
double bank.

A single bank SIMM may or may not use both sides of the module.  A
parity SIMM may use both sides if it needs extra real estate for the
parity chips.

In any case, our VLC has 3 banks of SIMMs from 3 different
manufacturers: DEC, DATARAM, IBM.  All of the SIMMs are double sided.

If the SIMM has 8 memory chips on one side, and then a couple more on
the back, it is a parity SIMM.  This describes the DEC and DATARAM SIMMs
we have.  The IBM SIMMs are rather odd in that they have 6 chips on each
side, but if you look at the chips, 8 are identical and 4 are different.

Good luck!

