I've seen this problem on a number of different system with various

non-Digital brand disk.  



The method to report SCSI Logical Unit Numbers (LUN) is different 

between SCSI-1 and SCSI-2 standard.  A common drive firmware bug is to 

report that the drive fully conforms to SCSI-2 standard, but still 

reports LUNs via the SCSI-1 method.  This confuses software resulting 

in the configuration of ghost LUNs 501-507.



You can report the problem to the drive manufacturer, however, you are

likely to get the response, "Hey, our drive works great under Windows95,

which is 99.99% of our market, and we don't care about the rest!"  

Perhaps Maxtor may be more helpful than another manufacturer that I 

reported a similar problem.  The problem must be fixed in the drive 

firmware, which present qualification issues for the manufacture, and

upgrade problems for the end user.



Alternatively, simply use only LUN 0 (DKA500) and ignore all other LUNs 

(DKA501-DKA507).  Most of the time, everything works O.K.



 

