	Do you have a reference to such scaling handy?  Being a bit
	coy there?  Look at your scaling there.  Single node UE6000 does
	31000, 4 nodes do 51000.   DU , no better.  Galaxy, much better.

> HA clustering is much easier to implement. Since
> it does not inflict any DLM overhead on the system
> only recovery traffic, it also does not require any data
> partitioning.
> 

	But what a waste.  More to the point... what clever marketing.
	HA clustering?  Is that an oxymoron?  Looks like one.  I mean
	how can something be part of a cluster if all it is doing is spinning
	a fan blade and heating up the room?

	There you go again with the data partitioning.  Thinking in 
	UNIX terms.  You might want to consider what a Galaxy system
	gains you.  Not only does it balance CPU and memory but it also
	balances I/O.  The I/O will migrate to the node that is most heavily
	accessing it.  MSCP serving will be greatly improved.  That coupled
	with the fact that lock traffic will occur over the cross-bar
	for instances local to a Wildfire and will have a very high-bandwidth
	low-latency interconnect for inter-nodal communication.

	Besides, the RDB folks have known for sometime how to minimize lock
	ping.  The key here is to raise PE1 so trees of a certain size
	will not migrate.  As trees grow on remote nodes they rightfully get
	re-mastered.  Not piddly ones.  Since the RDB and Oracle folks
	share dining areas , it probably wouldn't be much of a stretch
	to suppose Oracle locking will improve likewise even
	though they don't take advantage of the VMS built-ins.


