Subject: Re: Latency vs performance (was Re: Sun Servers take advantage of board local memory?)
Date: 1998/05/08
Message-ID: <6iv4qk$6a0$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 351435405
Distribution: inet
References: <35359B3A.2A830CB1@earthlink.net> <3548EC1B.41C6@radia1.com> <6iarfr$iq6$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com> <354A0344.B38B650C@Eng.Sun.Com>
Organization: Silicon Graphics, Inc.
Reply-To: mccalpin@engr.sgi.com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.hardware,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.unix.solaris,comp.arch,comp.sys.dec,comp.benchmarks


In article <354A0344.B38B650C@Eng.Sun.Com>,
ASHOK SINGHAL  <ashok.singhal@Eng.Sun.Com> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>> 
>> In article <3548EC1B.41C6@radia1.com>, Jim Tuccillo  <jjt@radia1.com> wrote:
>> >John McCalpin wrote:
>> 
>> Precisely.  There is no penalty in local memory latency as the size
>> of the machine is increased.
>
>
>We could also have arranged for Sun machines to have this property
>by forcing smaller systems to have longer latencies so that the
>bigger systems don't pay an "additional" penalty.
>That would, of course, have been a silly thing to do.

Yes, it would have been silly.

>But that is what SGI has done.  

No, not really, but this gets to historical issues.

SGI has never built the lowest latency systems in the
industry. The 195 MHz Origin2000 machines have a local latency 
that is about 1/3 of the latency of their predecessor, the
Power Challenge system, and the new 250 MHz Origin2000's are
down to about 1/4 of the Power Challenge latency -- all with 
the same cpu (the R10000).  Future R12000-based systems, and
subsequent ccNUMA machines will continue to reduce the latency.

A more historically accurate rendition of Ashok's comment would
be something along the lines of:

 "SGI missed the opportunity to build small systems with
  even lower latency, going instead with ccNUMA systems
  with moderate local latency and larger remote latency."

This is probably correct.  We could have built slightly better
performing small systems by optimizing for that special case.
What we did instead was use the same electronics in the small
systems and the large systems so that we could reduce the price
of both large and small systems, rather than increase the
performance of the small systems.


> Their small O2000 systems do indeed pay a large penalty in
> *local* latency for the ability to expand the system for no
> further local latency penalty (there are large increases in
> average latencies in most apps).  

This last statement is not generally true.  

Do "most" apps use more than 50% of the memory of a 4-cpu
(2-node) system?  Some certainly do, and for these, the portion
of the memory used that is over the 50% mark will have to be
allocated on the remote node, and suffer increased latency.  

However, it seems to me that "most" individual apps will fit in
local memory, especially if the machine is running on the order
of one application per cpu.  In that case, if "most" apps used
more memory than was available locally, the physical memory of
the machine would be oversubscribed, and the machine would thrash
horribly.  This is painful enough that most people manage to make
it not happen, either by serializing the execution of the
large-memory jobs, or by buying more RAM.
