I think you are missing the point: the integer/commercial
performance of the maxed out 4 way AS isn't much better than
the maxed out 4 way Sun and IBM systems, with the total cost of the
maxed out Sun and IBM systems which perform better than
2 way AlphaServers /600 being less than the 1 way AlphaServer.

System                                           CPUs  SpecIntRate FpRate
Digital Equipment Corp. AlphaServer 4100 5/600   1     169         262
Digital Equipment Corp. AlphaServer 4100 5/600   2     334         489
Digital Equipment Corp. AlphaServer 4100 5/600   4     657         858
Sun Microsystems Sun Ultra Enterprise 450/296MHz 4     422         561
IBM Corp RS/6000 F50/333MHz                      4     510         387


For the RS/6000 F50, you can get the system with 4 processors, 4.5GB
drive, CDROM, and 1GB of RAM for around $63000.  I took these prices
from a TPC-C report.  The system, which is 32 bit, has a max ram 
capacity of 3GB, and nine PCI slots (400MB/s I/O w/2 32 bit busses
and one 64 bit bus), 

For the Sun 450, based on a fall 1997 TPC report, I calculate the
cost for a system as $60000 for a similar 4 way configuration.


If you aren't look for top notch FP performance or the best single CPU 
performance, it is hard to justify the price of the AS4100, since you 
can get cheaper systems which perform better.  The only other Xes 
against the F50 is the fact that it is a 32 bit system and has a max 
ram capacity of 3GB.  However, I believe it is an upgradable system 
and will likely support 64 bit processors in the future.  However, 
since the AS4100 doesn't support more than 4GB of RAM, that reduces 
the advantage of a 64 bit OS.


Anil




