I wrote:
|  ...the Starfire and an APMP (Galaxy) box are different beasts. One is
|  a "shared nothing" model, with hardware-enforced instance independence,
|  and the other is designed to optionally allow "shared everything,"
|  trusting software to keep things properly partitioned.

Michael David Jones replied:
|  You may view "trusting software" as a feature. I've done professional
|  software development for 10 years including Unix (AIX) kernel
|  development, and I'd go for a hardware partitioned machine over a
|  software partitioned one six days a week and twice on Sundays.

I view it only as an alternative. It is a "feature" in the sense that it
allows
greater granularity in partitioning and greater resource sharing. It is a
liability for those whose experience has shown them that most system
failures come from faulty software more often than faulty hardware.

A customer whose VMS cluster (or Solaris box, for that matter) has
been up for three or four years may have developed enough faith in the
operating system that the advantages in flexibility and performance
outweigh the additional sense of security hardware gives. Only time
will tell...

