SEGV  <mlepage@cgocable.net> wrote:
>I'm sitting here looking at my unused copy of Visual Studio 5.0 for
>Windows, and thinking of the pathetic UNIX IDEs I have used in the past
>two years.
>
> - SparcWorks on Solaris
> - Softbench on HP-UX
> - ladebug etc. on Digital UNIX
>
...
>My question is, "Why is there nothing similar on UNIX?"

Lack of competition.  For a long time in the PC world, Microsoft,
Borland, Symantec, Watcom, Metaware, and a few others, were all fighting
it out in the developer tools market.  A nice looking and useful IDE was
one of the things some of them chose to compete on (Microsoft and
Borland and Symantec, specifically).  Microsoft has pretty much won the
PC compiler market, so the others are now competing in other areas.
E.g., Powersoft (which owns Watcom) has a really cool C++ RAD system
called Power++, and Borland has their C++Builder.  These go way beyond
Microsoft's MFC (and way beyond anything I've seen for Unix, either).

On Unix, there seems to be less competition.  In many cases, you've only
really got two choices: the vendor's tools, and the GNU tools.

--Tim Smith



