In article <6ianno$19lk$1@news.rchland.ibm.com>,
Del Cecchi <dcecchi@vnet.ibm.com> wrote:
> [asking about some features of the SGI Origin2000 ccNUMA machine...]
>
> Maybe I have asked this in the past, but how do you handle page
> faults?  Do you force the new page coming in to be local to the
> node that generated the page fault, or allow it to go anywhere,
> or is there some options in the page fault handler to choose?
>-- 
>Del Cecchi  
>cecchi@rchland

The default handler tries to allocate a page local to the cpu that
faulted.  If it is unable to find a free page of the correct size on
that node, it will start searching on neighboring nodes.  The free
page finder understands the network topology and will search all
the "1-hop-away" nodes before going to the "2-hop-away" nodes, etc.

When requesting pages of non-standard (i.e. large) size, there is
an additional degree of freedom.  You can give either smaller pages
locally, or the requested size remotely.  I don't know what the system
does by default -- I make the right decisions manually when I deal
with this problem.

You can change from the default "first touch" policy for allocation
to a "round-robin" policy simply by setting an environment variable.
The "round-robin" policy allocates pages around the set of nodes 
that have been requested for your job (usually the number of cpus
divided by two), so you still have a degree of locality.

If you want another policy, you have to read a lot of documentation
and build your own policy module.  I have never needed to do this,
but it is available to users.
