In article <3551597B.41C6@austin.unimelb.edu.au>, Paul Schleyer <pjs@austin.unimelb.edu.au> writes:
:Can anyone tell me which of these two options has the advantage:
:
:1- 500MHz DEC Personal Workstation 500au, no L2 cache.
:2- 433MHZ DEC Personal Workstation 433au with 2meg L2 cache. 
:
:The second option is actually more costly than the first, but I was
:wondering whether the performance increase was greater for MHz increase, 
:or for cache increase.

  "It depends".

  I can see no certain answer to this question, short of running your
  actual application(s) on the system and comparing the two.  (And even
  comparing benchmarks is questionable, unless you plan to use the system
  to run benchmarks.  Many benchmarks now fit into the cache on systems
  from DEC and other vendors, and this can obviously impact the results.)

  I would tend to *assume* that the system with L2 cache would have the
  advantage over the system with none in the general case -- due to the
  impact of main memory stalls on overall application performance, but
  I do know of specific applications that do "thrash cache" and thus run
  faster on systems without L2 cache.  And you may or many not be running
  "the general case"...

