Is it your assertion, then, that hardware is more reliable?  While
many of our system-level problems occur due to software problems, true
software engineering can be every bit as reliable as hardware
engineering.  And how do you expain things like the Intel hardware
bugs?  Ethernet controllers that don't follow the rules (ie, CSMA-CD
rules) and instead flood the network?  CPUs that eventually start
failing?

One thing I can say for software:  Once the algorithm is correct, it's
reliability doesn't deteriorate to the point where it stops working -
assuming all other aspects haven't changed.  Hardware, on the other
hand, does degrade over time so that, even if left in the exactly the
same configuration of hardware and software, it will eventually fail.
