>|>basically I use XEmacs to edit my source and make files, and the command
>|>line to compile. I debug with whatever debugger is best for the platform
>|>I am using that day.
>|>
>|>I understand that Visual Studio was created for the lowest common
>|>denominator (that's a fact, not an opinion), and that UNIX programmers
>|>are more comfortable with makefiles and command lines. But you have to
>|>admit that Visual Studio, and Metrowerks Codewarrior, are great IDEs.
>|>
>|>My question is, "Why is there nothing similar on UNIX?"

I think you'll find that ProDev on SGIs is quite wonderful.  I use vi and/or
XEmacs when editing source files, create my Makefile with XEmacs, and I'll do
a trial compile with XEmacs.  If it all builds, I'll flip over to my desktop
with ProDev, and workshop debugger (IMO one of the coolest pieces of Unix
software anywhere), the build tool and a bunch of watch/class browser windows
will be open and waiting to go.  I just hit enter on the ProDev executable box,
and it reloads the newly baked binary, reloads the changed source files, and
I'm ready to go.  ProDev on Irix sped up my development and more importantly
DEBUG time by several orders of magnitude.  We got a 30 day license and in 
those 30 days I managed to track down and nail quite a few linked list bugs
in our companies billing software.  Doing the same with GNU tools is impossible
on IRIX, and painful anywhere else.  If you're stuck with GNU based tools, DDD
isn't bad, but stick to vanilla C.  If you're doing C++, a real C++ compiler
and a debugger made for C++ (such as workshop) are unbeatable.




