Matthias Dolder wrote:

> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:

> >
> >
> > In otherwords be happy that Oracle may support locking
> > that minimises pinging but don't expect to be able to use
> > it.
>
> Please ? Could you explain  your above statement ?Block pinging is
> an Oracle phenomena when running OPS with hash
> locks enabled.  Probably you wanted to say
> that we can be glad that we support DBA locking in a
> DU / OPS environment, because it eliminates
> block pinging. Whether you apply hash locks
> (which can can produce bock pings) or dba locks
> (which do not block ping but have other disadvantages)
> is a matter of tuning. Both can be mixed in the same db.

But only if the application allows it and thats the rub.Lets take Baan for example. Baan do not support
Oracle Parallel Server. OPS and Baan may well work
fine as a combination, but most customers won't
go into production with an unsupported system.

So at a very basic level the Digital OPS TPC-C
result has no meaning at all to any Baan customers.
There are plenty of other applications that have
simular restrictions.

> > Please re read all information about clusters. I am
>
> > sure that Digital could if they wanted to run out a
>
> > massive TPC-C clustered number (TPC-C is easy
> > to partition) it will only interest customers who need
> > or can use a cluster and this a small subset of the
> > actual Database server market.
>
> Ah, now Harrison's benchmark laws again comeinto operation. Could it be that you started to feel
> that we are about to release a pretty decent cluster
> benchmark ? So everyone on the net immediately
> has to consider clustered tpc-c unimportant ? Just let
> people decide on their own. Matter of fact, tpc-c
> does allow clustered benchmarks, oracle happens
> to offer a parallel db and we took advantage.
> You more than often in recent postings asked for
> more recent benchmarks. By the time you read
> this you certainly have seen the new high watermark
> in tpc-c: 102'541.85 tpmC, 139.53 $/tpmC.
> And if cluster benchmarks are of so little interest,
> why did you do one ?

Re read my posts, I never said that there isno interest in clustered benchmarks. Customers
after all do use clusters, but they are in a minority.

What clustered TPC-C numbers do not prove is
anything about a platforms performance. They
are much less usefull as predictor of real
systems performance than even TPC-C because
in order to get the number Digital partitioned the
database which is easy to do for TPC-C but
much less practical in real applications.

If  you think that 100,000 or even 200,000
TPM using a cluster proves anything about
DU/8400 performance then think again. It proves
that Digital have the resources to put 8 8400s
and a bunch of disk in a benchmark center and
that is just about it. Many people would say
why so many boxes, Sun could probably do
it with under half as could HP if either had the
inclination.

Now you have done a cluster number how about
something a bit more usefull, like say a
decent SAP number, a decent Baan Number,
a single system TPC-C number etc etc etc.

How about say a clustered SAP number if
clusters really work as advertised by Digital.

What is most revealing is that Digital
have chosen just about the only public
database benchmark that is easy to
get to scale on a Cluster.

In England we have a saying one Swallow
does not make a summer. :)

> And btw, a business partner of us just brought
> to my attention that we do have a BaaN benchmark
> number of 5150 BaaN Reference Users (client/server).

This response probably illustrates Digtalsplight most graphically.

Baan has two types of database connection
the Baan level I driver and the Baan Level II
driver. The result you quote is using the level I
driver and its a slow result anyway. Sun, HP and
Sequent all had better numbers using the Level
I driver. Baan now promote the use of the level
II driver which is much less efficient though more
sophisticated and no Digital don't have a good
Baan result using the level II driver.

> Now you're switching to content free blah blah.

> What aboutkeeping up some minimum 'niveau' ? Everyone knows that
> Sun and HP currently have a larger market share than DU
> in the unix market. I understand that it does not feel
> comfortable to see DU steadily grow and in the meantime
> have become the size of a 3+b$ business instead of
> disappearing from the market. Alone in Swzl more than
> 100 Enterprises rely on DU/SAP to run their business.
> So somehow your statement was right, "describing
> DU as an up and coming Enterprise OS is laughable",
> it's not coming, it's here....
> And a free advice, do not underestimate Compaq....
>

But thats the whole point, DU growing is just
a marketing fiction. The latest market figures
show DU market share declining from 5.7% to
5.1% (HP and Sun have ~27% each) with DU
revenues down 7% for the full year. DU revenue
may have grown 11% in the last quarter but
even this is lower than the market leaders
Sun and HP.

Incedentally you are a master of understatement
Sun and HP have > 5 x the market share of Digital.

How can you justify something that has a negative
annual growth rate as up and coming.

Show me some independant numbers that show
DU actually gaining market share, my numbers
BTW were from Dataquest.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






