Graham Godfrey wrote:
> 
> I am attempting to upgrade a VAX 7640 to a VAX7650.  When I insert the
> new cpu into slot 4 of the LSB I get a console dump on power-up and do
> not get to >>>'s.  I can place a single CPU in slot 4 and get to the
> >>>'s ok.  My normal configuration and revisions are:
> 
> slot 0 = E2045-AA R07
> slot 1 = E2045-AA S06
> slot 2 = E2045-AA P07
> slot 3 = E2045-AA P07
> slot 4 = Empty / E2045-AA S06 (New upgrade)
> slot 5 = Empty
> slot 6 = E2043-BA
> slot 7 = E2043-BA
> slot 8 = E2044-AA
> 
> I can get a cpu working in slot 4 with another cpu in one of the other
> slots as long as I do not use the rev S06 CPU !!  My console version is
> V4.02-4835
> 
> My supplier said that DEC are going to replace all revision cpu's below
> R07!   If so, is this going to be an FCO or is it going to cost me?  If
> so how much?
> 
> Will I have to upgrade my cpu's before I can upgrade to a 7650? and use
> the S07!

Greeting,
I can help you on this problem. I have several 7xxx systems and I
upgrade them quite often. (1) I would suggest you trying to get the
newest firmware release which is  4.3-4842. This is on the VAX7000
console cd. Call DECdirect, and the cost is like $500.00 dollars (2)
Look at getting your "P07" rev. processors upgraded to the S06 or 07
level. The "S" and "R" hardware series seem to work ok together. You can
check with DEC which will be the most expensive or check with someone
like DecisionOne, less expensive. D1 has a repair shop that can upgrade
your cards and send them back to you. I would also check with any local
used computer brokers in your area that are on the Digital Dealers
Assocation's network. I upgraded my "P" cpu's many years ago.(3) your
process of puting in the cpu in slot 4 is correct and turn on the system
and wait untill your get the >>> prompt, then update the firmware via
the console cd and the ee prom and seeprom. Then you should be in
business. Make sure all your cpu's are at the same level of firmware or
you will get errors like: firmware mismatch on cpu xx, eeprom problems,
etc.. 

The last thing to check is put the "new" cpu in slot 1, by itself, a
single cpu setup and power on, and you should get a >>> with everything
ok, if not you have a bad cpu module. Then make sure you don't have a
bad slot on the backplane.

If I can help, let me know.
Regards,
Tim



