In article <6i7ost$rg9@flonk.uk.sun.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com#> writes:
> Terry Shannon wrote:

> > I can't explain that. Maybe someone from Sun can explain how Dynamic
> > System Domains make StarFire the greatest thing since sliced bread?

the best features of DR are used in association with alternate pathing (AP).
in the case of a hardware failure (correctable memory errors, bad sbus
card, dead gbic/fcom, etc.), DR allows you to take a system board out of 
a running configuration, while AP can take alternate paths to networks,
disks, etc..  you can power down the offending system board, pull it from
the machine, do the required service tasks, plug it back in, power it up,
and bring it back into the running machine without shutting down or rebooting.

cpu's are idled out, memory is evacuated, hardware peripheral adapters are
silenced and unloaded...  all through this nifty little ssp interface.  when
the board is ready to go back in, the steps are reversed, and the cpus,
memory, and adapters get punched back into the mix.

alternatively...  8)  say you are running your corporate financials on the
starfire.  end of quarter or end of fiscal, you can add in cpu's and memory
stolen from other domains for a temporary boost in processing power to
run reports, generate exports, etc..  after the crunch, add them back into
wherever you stole them from and everyone is happy.  no reboots or power-offs
required.

lots of folks are looking at the DSD features for server consolidation,
cluster-in-a-box, y2k testing, and data chunk moves.

it may not be the best thing since sliced bread, but it is a nifty resource
added into your IT arsenal.

bill.
#include <std-disclaimer.h>


> Don't need to just ask a StarFire Customer 70% use domains. Butseriously Dynamic
> Domains give them the same sort of functionallity
> as IBM LPARS and most of them want this sort of capability on
> a large server that is going to support multiple applications.
> 
> The benefits are:
> 
> Different times can be set for indevidual domains (now how
> would that be usefull.
> 
> Different OS releases or different patch levels of the same
> OS can be run on each domain.
> 
> Apps from one domain cannot steal resources from
> another domain.
> 
> But CPU's and Memory can be moved from domain
> to domain dynamically.
> 
> An example of this:
> 
> One customer I work for has a dual domain Starfire
> one domain is runs a high volume OLTP application
> and one runs a DSS database. For various good
> reasons they cannot run on one very large system.
> 
> This customer can do his batch processing in the
> DSS domain at night, then move CPU's and memory
> into the OLTP domain before the daily OLTP work
> load increases.
> 
> This is a simple configuration which would require
> two much larger separate systems to do the job
> without domains.
> 
> Regards
> Andrew Harrison
