In article <6ijto1$6uo@hatathli.csulb.edu>,
William Lane <wlane@engr.csulb.edu> wrote:  
> Just to be stupid Dr.  McCalpin but is the memory latency figure
> you're referring to the latency from when a valid address is
> detected to when we get data out from the RAM or when we get data
> into the CPU(s), I'd guess the latter by your "load to use"
> terminology. If true the memory latency time you quoted almost
> borders on the speed of an 8051 MCU!

For the Origin, the numbers are what we call a "pipeline restart"
latency.  It is hard to set of a measurement methodology to get
this number accurately, since the processor executes out-of-order
if the data dependencies allow it, but it has been done by Cristina
Hristea (was at MIT, now at SGI).

Basically, we start counting when the load that misses the caches
first hits an execute stage, and we stop counting when the first
instruction that uses the loaded data is able to execute.  You
can argue about whether to count the first and last cycles of
the above definition, but it does not change the numbers very much.

Alternate definitions that are in common use are:
	"pin-to-pin" which only measures the time for the 
		memory system to respond, and ignores getting
		in and out of the cpu.
	"lmbench/lat_mem_rd" which measures the time per iteration
		of dependent loads (chasing a chain of pointers).

"pin-to-pin" latency is always less than "pipeline restart" latency
which is always less than or equal to "lmbench/lat_mem_rd" latency.
