I am probably going to take a tangent from the original
intent of this thread... which I guess was to propose an IDE
development project...

SEGV <mlepage@cgocable.net> wrote:
 
> I understand that Visual Studio was created for the lowest common
> denominator (that's a fact, not an opinion), and that UNIX programmers
> are more comfortable with makefiles and command lines. But you have to
> admit that Visual Studio, and Metrowerks Codewarrior, are great IDEs.
> 
> My question is, "Why is there nothing similar on UNIX?"

First, a quick conjecture ...

Since UNIX development has been dominated up to now by fairly
well-seasoned developers with a lot of makefile comfort,
an IDE has less incremental value to them than to those in
the MS environment.

But there are some more critical issues ...

The commercial solutions from Sun, HP, etc. consume product
development dollars, and thus can take away resources from other
potentially burning issues.  If you have multiple products,
some which are good enough and some which are giving you a bad
name, then you put your dollars into fixing the ones giving
you a bad name (or you kill them altogether).

The result is that, unless the product is really a company
cash-cow, products which are good enough or pretty good don't
get any more polish.  If a product starts to fall behind its
desired industry position, it then gets funding to bring up
back to good enough.

Open source code does not suffer from this funding limitation.
As long as there are sharp users out there who are willing to
debug and improve, a software entity is able to march forward;
polish is always an option.

The problem with open source then becomes, how fast can it
march forward?  If your work force is composed of volunteers
with other academic or professional obligations, you can't
march as quickly as the commercial product.

This leads to the following observation:  commercial products
improve via multiple sprints; open source improves via
marathons.

> Even the free tools are not much better. Perhaps that's because they are
> volunteer efforts. [But then why can't Sun do better?] Perhaps it's
> because everyone's read "No Silver Bullet" and moved on to the
> "essential" tasks of development, leaving the "accidental" tasks like
> IDE behind.

I haven't read that paper in a long time, but I will agree
with the above observation.  [As for Sun, I personally believe
that the SPARCworks group fell victim to the commercial good
enough syndrome.]

At this point, I'm inclined to think that open source projects could
greatly benefit by funding a couple of people.

    1.  architect - a person who understands the
        requirements of a product, how it ought to be
        implemented, what risks need to be shaken out where
        in the development cycle, and can communicate all this
        to willing developers.

    2.  gatekeeper - a person who understands the mind of
        the architect, provides/collects guidelines for code
        integration criteria, and actually integrates changes
        into the code base.

Depending on project size, these two people may be one
and the same person.  Have such people on a full-time or
even half-time basis should speed things along immensely.
(The trick, of course, is how to fund this.  I'm still trying
to figure that out.)

Now... back to what may be the original intent of the thread.

> If I'm missing out on an excellent free/commercial IDE, please let me
> know. If not, why can't one be built? Make a clone of Visual Studio,
> fixing its annoyances at the same time. Make it fully graphical for the
> neophyte developer, but unleash the power beneath for the scripting
> guru. Build it out of standard components: GNU compilers, make, CVS,
> etc. Use the GTK toolkit, eventually provide a visual GUI editor.
> 
> Does this sound farfetched? Maybe, maybe not. I'm just trying to feel
> this idea out. Is there a project like this going on now? If not, is
> this an idea whose time has come?

I don't think this is far-fetched.

The one thing I think Visual Studio does poorly is .mak file
generation.  Partly, I think it is simply a complex problem;
partly, I think MS simply lost its way and are using Visual
Studio to hide their own mess.  I think we can do better.

