References: <35359B3A.2A830CB1@earthlink.net> <35487AF7.41C6@radia1.com> <6iam2p$fve$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com> <3548EC1B.41C6@radia1.com> <6iarfr$iq6$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Sun Microsystems
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.hardware,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.unix.solaris,comp.arch,comp.sys.dec,comp.benchmarks


John McCalpin wrote:
> 
> In article <3548EC1B.41C6@radia1.com>, Jim Tuccillo  <jjt@radia1.com> wrote:
> >John McCalpin wrote:
> 
> Precisely.  There is no penalty in local memory latency as the size
> of the machine is increased.


We could also have arranged for Sun machines to have this property
by forcing smaller systems to have longer latencies so that the
bigger systems don't pay an "additional" penalty.
That would, of course, have been a silly thing to do.

It's similar to the old trick with "scaling".  You can get anything to
"scale" better if you make the base case slower.

But that is what SGI has done.  Their small O2000 systems do indeed
pay a large penalty in *local* latency for the ability to expand
the system for no further local latency penalty (there are large
increases
in average latencies in most apps).  The local 290 ns "restart" latency
on a 2-way O2000 is much worse than that of a 4-way E450.  It is even
worse
than a much larger Sun E4000 or E6000 that has to traverse over a
2-level hierarchy of shared buses (UPA and Gigaplane) to another board
and back.

So yes, they pay no additional in local latency for scaling to bigger
systems, but a large part of that reason is because they have a high
local latency for small systems to begin with.

Ashok Singhal
Sun Microsystems



