Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

>OK, no reboot makes sense. But what about applications and users? Near
>as I can tell; apps, drivers, and users go down for several seconds
>during the reconfiguration.

Not really; what needs to happen is that memory, CPUs and devices that are
in use are taken off-line; so the domain that is reconfigured will suddenly
lose some CPUs and the paging algorithm has to clean the memory on
the boards that are being moved.  Assming that you move stuff from
domains that have plenty, this really shouldn't hinder current processing
very much,

>Moreover, I get the impression that DSD has coarser granularity than
>does DEC's APMP approach. With DSD, the entire domain must move, not an
>individual CPU.

It's not domain level granularity, it's board level.  So you need to
move an entire board.  This is because it's implemented in hardware on
the back plane and not on the system boards.

You can use something like processor sets to move single CPUs within the
same domain and assign them to different tasks.  But such software solutions,
while worthwhile in practice, don't offer the fault resistance the hardware
solution gives you (if a CPU fails, only your domain is a gone because of
hardware fault isolation; in a software only solution, a CPU fault in one
domain can affect all other domains in unpredicatable ways; e.g., the
Pentium LOCK bug locked up the entire bus.)

Domains are not the entities that move; they can shrink and grow (or
go out and spring into existance)

Casper
