Lupe Christoph wrote:
> 
> Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
> 
> >Moreover, I get the impression that DSD has coarser granularity than
> >does DEC's APMP approach. With DSD, the entire domain must move, not an
> >individual CPU.
> 
> The granularity is system boards (up to four CPUs each). This is because
> the domain feature is implemented in the crossbar. And the crossbar
> talks to entire system boards.
> 
> Bad:    High granularity
> Good:   Guaranteed independence of domains.

Independence of domains is good, but what's so bad about high
granularity? Having used a Galaxy system, I find that it's nice
to be able to migrate a single CPU from one instance to another,
on the fly.

I can't see any problem with that.

Cheers,

Terry Shannon




