In article <1998May2.110235.1@eisner>, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:

> In article <casper.894109379@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>, Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.Com (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer) writes:
>> It's not domain level granularity, it's board level.  So you need to
>> move an entire board.  This is because it's implemented in hardware on
>> the back plane and not on the system boards.
> 
> So I gather a domain is a configuration concept, corresponding to the
> Galaxy term "instance".  A single collection of memory shared by all
> the processors belonging to that domain.  Is that correct?
> 
> So how many processors are on an entire board (and move together between
> domains) ?

	4 processors.  So if you have an 8 processor domain, it loses half
	its power to increase the power of another domain.  So if you have
	a node that needs some power it will punish another node.  Of course
	it could be argued you may want to have 12, 16 etc. CPU nodes...
	
	Again, compared to a Galaxy solution, Galaxy seems more flexible.

	Strength doesn't appear to load balance on the fly.  Appears to
	have the strength of reconfiguring at night.

